Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS — enable real-time content updates in the application when a CMS editor modifies content through Contentstack's Live Preview feature, so that editors see their changes reflected on the page without a manual refresh.

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source that feeds all 236+ content consumers via useContent → useContentstackContext. It currently holds content as a static memoized prop (defaultContent) with no mechanism to receive real-time updates from Contentstack's Live Preview SDK. Enabling Live Preview requires this provider to subscribe to SDK events and re-render the tree with updated content — no other site can accomplish this because no other code sits between the CMS data source and the React context.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the static useMemo with useState initialized from defaultContent. Add a useEffect that calls ContentstackLivePreview.init() from @contentstack/live-preview-utils (the standard Contentstack Live Preview SDK) and subscribes to its onEntryChange callback, which receives the updated entry payload. In the callback, deep-merge the incoming live preview data into the current content state and call the setter to trigger a re-render of all consumers. The useMemo on line 56-61 becomes: const [content, setContent] = useState(defaultContent); useEffect(() => { setContent(defaultContent); }, [defaultContent]); useEffect(() => { const unsub = ContentstackLivePreview.onEntryChange((liveData) => { setContent(prev => deepMerge(prev, liveData)); }); return unsub; }, []); and the context value becomes { content }. Import ContentstackLivePreview from '@contentstack/live-preview-utils' and deepMerge from the existing deepMerge utility if one exists in the repo, otherwise use a simple spread-based merge. This is the minimal change: one provider file, no changes to any of the 236 consumers.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- After an editor saves a content change in the CMS, the updated content appears on the page without the tester manually refreshing or reloading it.
- Every place on the page where the edited content is displayed shows the new value after the change is saved in the CMS.
- Content on the page renders correctly and completely, with no Live Preview artifacts such as editing overlays, badges, or debug markup visible in the normal view.
- When the page is opened normally (not launched from the CMS preview), it displays the published content and behaves as it always has, with no preview-only UI appearing.
- When only some content is edited in the CMS, only the corresponding portions of the page update; sections whose content was not changed remain as they were.

